const PROSE: Record<string, Prose> = {"east-continues-1453":{"body":"The Roman Empire did not end in 476; it lost its western provinces. The eastern half — the richer half, the half Theodosius's division had endowed with Egypt, Syria, Asia Minor, and the short Danube frontier — carried on without interruption: the same offices descended from Augustus, the same law, the same Senate, the same taxes, ruled from the New Rome that Constantine had planted on the Bosporus precisely because the east was where the money was. Its citizens called themselves Romaioi — Romans — and their state the empire of the Romans, in Greek, until the fifteenth century. No one who lived in it ever heard the word \"Byzantine\": that name was coined by a German scholar, Hieronymus Wolf, in 1557, a full century after the empire's extinction.\n\nWhy did the east survive what killed the west? Structure, not virtue. It held the tax engines of Egypt and Syria, so it could always rebuild armies or simply pay enemies to leave — the subsidy was a weapon the broke west never had. Its exposed European frontier was short, and behind it stood Constantinople, ringed by the Theodosian Walls, which defeated every siege for eight hundred years. When the fifth-century storms came, the east could buy, divert, or absorb them; the west had to fight them with an empty treasury, and couldn't.\n\nThe next millennium was no serene coda. Justinian retook Africa and Italy in the sixth century; the seventh brought near-death — Persia at the walls, then the Arab conquests stripping Egypt, Syria, and Africa within a generation — and the empire contracted to an Anatolian-Balkan core, Greek-speaking and embattled, yet still Rome to itself and its neighbors. It recovered mightily by the eleventh century, was broken not by infidels but by the Fourth Crusade in 1204, when western Christians sacked Constantinople and dismembered the state; restored itself in 1261 as a shadow; and ended on May 29, 1453, when the Ottoman army breached the land walls and the last emperor — Constantine XI, eighty-eighth in a line from Augustus — died fighting at the breach. Even then the claim did not quite die: Mehmed the Conqueror took the title Kayser-i Rum, Caesar of Rome.\n\nCall the span from the first Augustus to the last Constantine what it is: roughly 1,480 years of continuous Roman statehood. The \"fall of Rome\" of 476 is the story of half an empire, told by the heirs of the half that fell.","whyItMatters":"Whether you count Roman history as ending in 476 or 1453 is not trivia — it decides what you think the empire was. The habit of writing the east out of the story was itself a historical act: western Europe's popes and emperors needed the Roman inheritance vacant so they could claim it, and early modern scholars obliged by renaming the surviving empire \"Byzantine,\" as if it were some other, lesser thing. Take the empire at its own word and the shape of the whole subject changes — Rome becomes a Mediterranean state that lost its western half in the fifth century and its life in the fifteenth, and every \"fall of Rome\" argument becomes an argument about half a patient who lived another thousand years.","sources":[{"author":"Anthony Kaldellis","work":"Romanland","note":"The forceful modern case that \"Byzantium\" was simply Rome, and that denying its Romanness is a western tradition, not a fact."},{"author":"Judith Herrin","work":"Byzantium: The Surprising Life of a Medieval Empire","note":"An accessible survey of the millennium the label \"epilogue\" unfairly compresses."},{"author":"Marcellinus Comes","work":"Chronicle","note":"An eastern Roman announcing the western empire's death — proof that the surviving half saw itself as the empire, not a successor."}],"misconception":{"claim":"The Byzantine Empire was a separate, Greek empire that came after Rome.","correction":"It was the Roman Empire — in unbroken legal and institutional continuity from Augustus — and its citizens called themselves Romans to the end. \"Byzantine\" was coined in 1557, a century after the empire fell; no one who lived under it ever used the word."}},"justinian-527ce":{"body":"Fifty years after the western throne lapsed, the empire came back for its provinces. Justinian — a Latin-speaking Balkan soldier's nephew who reigned in Constantinople from 527 to 565 — sent his general Belisarius against Vandal Africa in 533, and the kingdom that had defied two empires for a century collapsed in a single season: two battles, Carthage retaken, the Vandal king paraded in a triumph. Italy looked equally easy at first — Sicily and Naples fell fast, Rome opened its gates in 536 — and then was not: the Ostrogoths fought back, and the Gothic War ground on for nearly twenty years. Rome was besieged five times, its aqueducts cut, its population scattered; the war that reclaimed Italy for the empire wrecked it more thoroughly than the fifth century ever had. In 554 Italy was Roman again, and ruined. The Senate of Rome, which had survived Alaric, Gaiseric, and Odoacer, faded out of existence within a generation; the consulship, older than the Republic's wars with Veii, had already been allowed to lapse in 541.\n\nTwo other legacies outweighed the maps. First, the law: Justinian's commission under Tribonian compressed a millennium of Roman jurisprudence into the Corpus Iuris Civilis — Code, Digest, Institutes — completed by 534. Second, the pandemic: in 541 bubonic plague reached Egypt and swept the Mediterranean, the first appearance of Yersinia pestis in Eurasian history. Its mortality is fiercely debated — estimates run from grave to catastrophic — but it struck an empire stretched to the limit of its resources, and the recurrences ran for two centuries. Within three years of Justinian's death the Lombards were in Italy; within a century the Arab conquests had taken Egypt, Syria, and then Africa itself. The reconquest's territorial gains proved a high-water mark, not a restoration.","whyItMatters":"Justinian settles, in one reign, both halves of the fall-of-Rome argument. Africa retaken in a season proves the contingency school's point — the western collapse was not fated, since the empire demonstrably had the power to reverse it. Italy proves the other point: the recovery destroyed what it recovered, and the peninsula that had passed to Odoacer intact was left depopulated and broken by its liberation. And the durable conquest turned out to be the one made of text. The Corpus Iuris, rediscovered in eleventh-century Italy, became the foundation of continental law; Rome's most successful annexation of the modern world was carried out by a book.","sources":[{"author":"Procopius","work":"Wars and Secret History","note":"The same eyewitness wrote the official war narrative and a private character assassination of Justinian — a source that impeaches itself, usefully."},{"author":"Kyle Harper","work":"The Fate of Rome","note":"Puts the 541 plague and climate shocks at the center of the sixth-century unraveling — nature as a protagonist alongside strategy."},{"author":"Peter Sarris","work":"Justinian: Emperor, Soldier, Saint","note":"A recent full portrait balancing the law, the wars, and the pandemic."}],"misconception":{"claim":"Justinian's reconquest restored the Roman west.","correction":"It restored territory, briefly and at ruinous cost. Italy was devastated by twenty years of war and lost to the Lombards within three years of Justinian's death; Africa held longer but fell to the Arab conquests in the following century. The reconquest's lasting achievement was legal, not territorial: the Corpus Iuris Civilis."}},"why-west-fell-565ce":{"body":"Why did the western empire end? Start with what this timeline itself shows. The division of 395 left the west the empire's long frontier and its thin tax base. The crossings of 406 turned Gaul and Spain from revenue provinces into war zones. The settlement of 418 established that armies could be paid in provinces — a solution that consumed the thing it defended. The loss of Africa in 439 cut the fiscal jugular, after which every failure is downstream of insolvency: no money, no army; no army, no reconquest; no reconquest, no money. The Huns accelerated everything twice — by pushing peoples across the frontier, and then by evaporating and stranding the west's balance-of-power strategy. When the joint expedition burned at Cape Bon in 468, the arithmetic closed. Nothing in that chain requires decadence, lead pipes, or divine judgment.\n\nBut describing the mechanism does not settle the meaning, and here the professionals genuinely divide. One school — Peter Heather, Bryan Ward-Perkins — insists on the word \"fall\": a functioning state was destroyed by armed outsiders, and the archaeology of the aftermath shows real collapse, in pottery, coinage, building, literacy, even the size of cattle. Ward-Perkins in particular argues that post-Roman material life regressed to pre-Roman levels, and that calling this \"transformation\" launders a catastrophe. The other school — Peter Brown, Walter Goffart — replies that \"fall\" is the wrong lens: Late Antiquity was a creative religious and cultural transformation, the \"barbarians\" were mostly accommodated immigrants absorbed through Roman fiscal machinery, and the world of 550 grew out of the world of 400 without any single dying moment. On Goffart's reading, the settlements were Roman policy that eventually outgrew Roman control — an experiment, not an invasion.\n\nNotice that each side is answering a different question. Ask what happened to the western Roman state, and it died — visibly, datably, in the fifth century. Ask what happened to the people, and they mostly stayed put, kept their law and their religion, and became something else by degrees. Both answers are correct. The argument is about which question matters more, and that is a judgment, not a fact.","whyItMatters":"This closing argument is the app's real lesson: history at this scale is not a settled record but a live dispute conducted over evidence, and the fall of Rome is its greatest case study. Every century has refought this question in its own image — Gibbon blamed Christianity, nationalists credited vigorous Germans, and the current debate between catastrophe and transformation tracks our own anxieties about state failure and migration. Learn the mechanism, by all means: division, breach, settlement, Africa, insolvency. But learn equally that \"fell\" versus \"transformed\" is an argument about what an empire is — and that fifteen hundred years of hindsight have not resolved it, because it is not resolvable by hindsight alone.","sources":[{"author":"Bryan Ward-Perkins","work":"The Fall of Rome and the End of Civilization","note":"The sharpest modern case for a real, material fall — argued from potsherds and roof tiles rather than battles."},{"author":"Peter Brown","work":"The World of Late Antiquity","note":"The founding text of the transformation school; reframes the era as creative change, and barely uses the word \"decline\"."},{"author":"Walter Goffart","work":"Barbarians and Romans","note":"Source of the famous verdict that the fall was an \"imaginative experiment that got a little out of hand\" — the settlement system as Roman policy, not conquest."}],"misconception":{"claim":"Rome fell because of decadence — or lead pipes, or Christianity sapping its strength.","correction":"Every single-cause explanation fails the same test: the east. It was richer, more urban, more Christian, drank from the same pipes — and lasted another thousand years. What differed was structure: the west had the long frontier, the thin tax base, and after 439, no Africa. The fall is a story about revenue and armies, not virtue."}}};
export default PROSE;
